; Map - an open-addressing hash map from `Int` keys to `Int` values, in
; Axiom.
;
; A `Map` is the address of a six-word header:
;
;     word 0 : len    - live entries
;     word 1 : cap    - slots, always a power of two
;     word 2 : keys   - address of cap words
;     word 3 : vals   - address of cap words
;     word 4 : states - address of cap *bytes*, one tag per slot
;     word 5 : used   - live entries plus tombstones
;
; Values are machine words, so the same code maps `Int -> Int`,
; `Int -> Str`, and `Int -> Vec` (see `Vec`'s module comment on uniform
; representation).
;
; WHY A SEPARATE STATE ARRAY, AND NOT A SENTINEL KEY. The obvious saving
; is to reserve one key value - 0, or the most negative `Int` - to mean
; "empty" and drop the third array. That is wrong here: keys are
; arbitrary `Int`s, and the callers this is for include a compiler
; mapping node ids and hash values to entries, where 0 and INT_MIN are
; both legitimate keys. A reserved sentinel would silently lose one key
; out of 2^64, and the bug would surface as a missing symbol in a program
; that happened to hash to it. One extra *byte* per slot buys a map that
; is total over the key space - and the byte width is not thrift but
; throughput: the state is the word every probe loads and branches on,
; so at 2^21 slots the state array is 2 MB of bytes against 16 MB of
; words, and it is the difference between probing mostly in cache and
; probing mostly in DRAM. Keys and values stay full words; their loads
; happen only when the state byte has already said the slot matters.
;
; The state tags are 0 = empty, 1 = live, 2 = tombstone, with empty as 0
; deliberately: `memAlloc` zeroes, so a fresh table is correctly "all
; empty" with no initialisation pass.
;
; DELETE IS SUPPORTED, via tombstones. `used` counts live entries *plus*
; tombstones and it is `used`, not `len`, that drives growth. That is not
; an optimisation, it is the correctness condition: linear probing
; terminates only because every probe sequence eventually reaches an
; empty slot, and a workload that alternates insert and remove converts
; empty slots into tombstones without changing `len`. Sizing on `len`
; would let such a workload fill the table with tombstones and then loop
; forever. A rehash discards tombstones, which is what recovers the
; space.
;
; LEAK ON GROWTH: rehashing allocates three new arrays and abandons the
; old three, because there is no `free`. Same trade, and same reasoning,
; as `Vec`; see that module's comment.
(import Mem)
(import Vec)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The hash
;
; WHY NOT `key % cap`. The identity hash is not a hash. `cap` is a power
; of two, so `key % cap` keeps only the low bits of the key, and the keys
; a compiler actually stores are not random in their low bits: pointers
; and arena offsets are 8- or 16-byte aligned, so the low three or four
; bits are always zero and 7/8 of the table is unreachable; sequential
; node ids inserted in order map to sequential slots, which turns linear
; probing's clustering behaviour from "rare" into "guaranteed". The hash
; has to move entropy from the whole key into the low bits before the
; mask sees them.
;
; WHAT THIS IS: murmur3's 64-bit finaliser (fmix64) - xor-shift,
; multiply, xor-shift, multiply, xor-shift. Five cheap operations, no
; division, and two properties that matter here:
;
;   - it is a bijection on the 64-bit key space, so distinct keys have
;     distinct hashes and a table-index collision is only ever the slot
;     mask's doing, never the hash's;
;   - it avalanches: every input bit flips every output bit with
;     probability near 1/2, so the low bits the mask keeps carry entropy
;     from the whole key.
;
; WHY NOT A UNIVERSAL MULTIPLY-MOD-PRIME FAMILY. This file used to hash
; with h(k) = (A0*l0 + A1*l1 + A2*l2 + C) mod (2^31 - 1) over 22-bit
; limbs - chosen when Axiom had no `^` or `>>` operators, and argued
; for by the family's provable 2-universality. Both premises rotted.
; The operators exist now. And the guarantee never applied: it is a
; bound over *randomly drawn* multipliers, while these were fixed - and
; worse, for keys below 2^22 two of the three limbs are constant, so
; the hash was *affine* in the key. An affine map sends sequential keys
; onto interleaved arithmetic progressions mod cap, and linear probing
; over that structure degenerates from occasional collisions into
; guaranteed pile-ups. Measured on 10^6 sequential keys at cap 2^21:
; 70,813,730 total probe steps - 71 per insert - against 1,457,580
; (1.46 per insert) for this finaliser. That 48x in probe volume was
; nearly the whole of the 15x gap to Rust's HashMap this module used
; to measure, misattributed for a while to allocator and layout costs
; that profiling then exonerated one by one.
;
; THE CONSTANTS ARE FIXED, NOT SEEDED. A per-process seed is what makes
; a map resistant to an adversary who feeds it keys that all land in
; one slot. These are hardcoded, so this map is *not* DoS-resistant.
; That is deliberate: `scripts/check-reproducible.sh` requires two runs
; of the compiler to emit byte-identical IR, and any output that depends
; on iteration order over a map would vary run to run with a seeded hash.
; Determinism is the requirement; DoS resistance is not, because the
; adversary would have to be the source file the user asked to compile.
; It is the same trade rustc makes by using FxHash.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; 2^31 - 1, a Mersenne prime. No longer used by `mapHash` itself; kept
; because `Intern`'s polynomial string hash reduces modulo it, and a
; prime modulus is what makes that polynomial hash sound.
(pub :: mapHashPrime Int)

(pub fn (mapHashPrime) 2147483647)

; fmix64's two multipliers, written as the signed 64-bit values whose
; bit patterns are 0xff51afd7ed558ccd and 0xc4ceb9fe1a85ec53. The
; multiplication wraps modulo 2^64 - LLVM `mul` - so only the bit
; pattern matters, not the sign.
(:: mapMixC1 Int)

(fn (mapMixC1) (- 0 49064778989728563))

(:: mapMixC2 Int)

(fn (mapMixC2) (- 0 4265267296055464877))

; The unsigned 33-bit right shift fmix64 calls for. Axiom's `>>` is an
; arithmetic shift - it fills with the sign bit - so a negative operand
; would smear ones into the top of the result. Masking to the 31 bits a
; 33-position shift can actually carry (bits 33..63 of the input) makes
; it exactly the logical shift: the sign-fill lands outside the mask.
(:: mapShr33 (-> Int Int))

(fn (mapShr33 h) (& (>> h 33) 2147483647))

; Hash `key` to a value in [0, 2^63).
;
; fmix64, exactly: xor-shift by 33, multiply, xor-shift by 33,
; multiply, xor-shift by 33. The final mask clears the sign bit so the
; documented range is non-negative - it costs one bit of the hash,
; and `mapSlotOf` keeps only low bits anyway.
;
; Zero is a fixed point: `(mapHash 0)` is 0, because every stage maps
; zero to zero. That is a known property of fmix64 and is harmless
; here - key 0 probes from slot 0, like any other key probes from its
; hash - but it is worth knowing when reading pinned values in tests.
(pub :: mapHash (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (mapHash key)
  (let ((a (* (^ key (mapShr33 key)) mapMixC1)))
    (let ((b (* (^ a (mapShr33 a)) mapMixC2)))
      (& (^ b (mapShr33 b)) 9223372036854775807)
    )
  )
)

; The slot `key` probes first, in [0, cap).
;
; The `< 0` branch is unreachable given `mapHash`, and it is here anyway.
; A negative index is not a wrong answer, it is a wild store into the
; arena below the keys array - memory corruption in a module whose whole
; job is to be trusted by everything above it. `Int` is a signed 64-bit
; two's-complement value and `%` is `srem`, which preserves the sign of
; the dividend, so the distance between "the hash went negative" and "the
; map scribbles on unrelated allocations" is exactly this test. Guarding
; costs one compare per probe and removes the class of bug entirely.
; `cap` is a power of two - every table is sized by `mapRoundUpPow2`
; and grown by doubling - so the reduction is a mask rather than a
; division, with identical results for the non-negative hash this
; receives.
(pub :: mapSlotOf (-> Int Int Int))

(pub fn (mapSlotOf key cap)
  (let ((h (& (mapHash key) (- cap 1))))
    (if (< h 0)
      (+ h cap)
      h
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Slot states
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Empty is 0 so that `memAlloc`'s zeroing initialises the table.
(:: mapStateEmpty Int)

(fn (mapStateEmpty) 0)

(:: mapStateLive Int)

(fn (mapStateLive) 1)

; A slot that held an entry that was removed. It must not stop a probe -
; the entry that followed it may have been placed past it - but it may be
; reused by an insert. See the module comment on why tombstones count
; toward growth.
(:: mapStateTomb Int)

(fn (mapStateTomb) 2)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Construction
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The slot count `mapNew` starts with, and the floor for every table.
;
; Eight, and never smaller: at the 0.5 load factor below, an 8-slot table
; holds 4 entries before rehashing, which covers the many tiny maps a
; compiler makes (a match arm's bindings, a module's exports) in one
; allocation. It must also be at least 2 for `mapWithCapacity`'s doubling to
; make progress and for a probe to have somewhere to go.
(:: mapDefaultCap Int)

(fn (mapDefaultCap) 8)

; An empty `Map` with `mapDefaultCap` slots.
;
; Deliberately *not* `(mapWithCapacity mapDefaultCap)`: that would ask
; for room for 8 entries and so allocate 16 slots. The two functions
; measure different things - `mapNew` names a slot count, and
; `mapWithCapacity` names an entry count - and conflating them made
; `mapNew` quietly twice as large as its own documented default.
(pub :: mapNew Int)

(pub fn (mapNew) (mapAllocTable mapDefaultCap 0))

; An empty `Map` whose VALUES it owns a share of: the value array
; carries the array form, so `mapFree` releases every value in it. Keys
; stay `Int`s and stay a leaf, which is what they are - `mapInsert`'s
; key parameter is `Int`, not a type variable.
(pub :: mapNewRefVals Int)

(pub fn (mapNewRefVals) (mapAllocTable mapDefaultCap 1))

; An empty `Map` sized so that `want` entries fit without rehashing.
;
; The requested count is doubled before rounding up to a power of two,
; because `want` entries at load factor 0.5 need 2*want slots. A caller
; that says `(mapWithCapacity 100)` means "I am about to insert 100
; things"; sizing the table at 128 and rehashing at 64 would make that
; request a lie.
(pub :: mapWithCapacity (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (mapWithCapacity want) (mapSized want 0))

; `mapWithCapacity`'s owning twin. See `mapNewRefVals`.
(pub :: mapWithCapacityRefVals (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (mapWithCapacityRefVals want) (mapSized want 1))

(:: mapSized (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (mapSized want refs)
  (mapAllocTable
    (mapRoundUpPow2
      (*
        (if (< want 1)
          1
          want
        )        2
      )
    )    refs
  )
)

; `n` rounded up to a power of two, at least `mapDefaultCap`.
;
; Powers of two are required, not preferred: `mapSlotOf` reduces with
; `%`, and a non-power-of-two `cap` would still work but would cost a
; real division per probe instead of a mask once the optimiser sees the
; constant. It also keeps `mapWithCapacity`'s doubling exact.
(pub :: mapRoundUpPow2 (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (mapRoundUpPow2 n)
  (if (>= mapDefaultCap n)
    mapDefaultCap
    (mapRoundUpPow2From mapDefaultCap n)
  )
)

(:: mapRoundUpPow2From (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (mapRoundUpPow2From cap n)
  (if (>= cap n)
    cap
    (mapRoundUpPow2From (* cap 2) n)
  )
)

; A header over three freshly zeroed arrays: `cap` words of keys, `cap`
; words of values, `cap` *bytes* of states - plus `len`, `used` and the
; flag saying whether this table owns a share of every value in it.
(:: mapAllocTable (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (mapAllocTable cap refs)
  ; Words 2, 3 and 4 are the map (28 = bits 2/3/4), so the
  ; header's death takes all three arrays with it - and each is
  ; retained once here for the reason `Vec.vecBuild` gives: a
  ; block the allocator answers has a count of zero, and release
  ; steps over zero.
  (let (
    (m (memAllocMapped 56 28))
    (keys (memAlloc (* cap 8)))
    (vals (memAlloc (* cap 8)))
    (states (memAlloc cap))
  )
    {
      (__retain m)
      (__retain keys)
      (__retain vals)
      (__retain states)
      (if (== refs 1)
        (memMarkArray vals)
        vals
      )
      (memSetWord m 0 0)
      (memSetWord m 1 cap)
      (memSetWord m 2 keys)
      (memSetWord m 3 vals)
      (memSetWord m 4 states)
      (memSetWord m 5 0)
      ; WORD 6, for the reason `Vec.vecBuild` gives at its word 3: the
      ; data block's shape bit answers the RUNTIME's question and is
      ; ambiguous against any allocator with a different clamp - it was
      ; a SIGSEGV under the committed seed, on a table big enough for
      ; its value array to pass 16,384 words. The map says what it is
      ; itself. Seven words in an eight-word block; word 7 is spare.
      (memSetWord m 6 refs)
      m
    }
  )
)

; Hand `m` back: the three arrays go with it, and on a `mapNewRefVals`
; table so does one share of every value still in it. Answers 0, as
; `Vec.vecFree` does and for the same reason.
(pub :: mapFree (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (mapFree m)
  {
    (__release m)
    0
  }
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Accessors
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: mapLen (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (mapLen m) (memGetWord m 0))

(pub :: mapCap (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (mapCap m) (memGetWord m 1))

; Slots that are live or tombstoned. Exposed because it is the number
; that explains a rehash, and a test that could not see it would have to
; infer growth from timing.
(pub :: mapUsed (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (mapUsed m) (memGetWord m 5))

; Hand back the share of the value in slot `i` and zero the word. A
; no-op on a `mapNew` table, which owns nothing it holds.
(:: mapDropVal (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (mapDropVal m i)
  (if (mapOwnsVals m)
    (let (
      (vals (memGetWord m 3))
      (old (memGetWord (memGetWord m 3) i))
    )
      {
        (memSetWord vals i 0)
        (__release old)
        m
      }
    )
    m
  )
)

; Whether this table owns a share of every value it holds - the
; `mapNewRefVals` half. Word 6 of the header, and not a test of the
; value array's shape word: see `mapAllocTable`.
(pub :: mapOwnsVals (-> Int Bool))

(pub fn (mapOwnsVals m) (== (memGetWord m 6) 1))

; Read the key, or the value, out of slot `i`.
;
; `pub` since 2026-09-03, and `mapStateAt` below deliberately is NOT.
; These two are the whole of what iterating a table needs to READ; the
; state byte is how it decides WHICH slots to read, and that question
; is answered by `mapLiveFrom` instead. Exporting the state array as
; well would put tombstones - an artifact of how delete keeps linear
; probing terminating - into the surface a caller has to understand,
; and there is nothing a caller could correctly do with a `2` that
; `mapLiveFrom` does not already do for it.
;
; Neither bounds-checks. A slot index that did not come from
; `mapLiveFrom` is the caller's error, the same way `vecGet`'s is.
(pub :: mapKeyAt (-> Int Int Int))

(pub fn (mapKeyAt m i) (memGetWord (memGetWord m 2) i))

; The value in slot `i`. See `mapKeyAt` above for the bounds rule
; and why `mapStateAt` is not exported beside these two.
(pub :: mapValAt (-> Int Int Int))

(pub fn (mapValAt m i) (memGetWord (memGetWord m 3) i))

(:: mapStateAt (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (mapStateAt m i) (memGetByte (memGetWord m 4) i))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Probing
;
; Linear probing - step 1, wrapping at `cap` - rather than quadratic
; probing or double hashing. Linear probing has the worst theoretical
; clustering of the three and the best cache behaviour by a wide margin,
; because a probe run is a contiguous forward scan of one cache line's
; worth of keys. Holding the load factor at 0.5 (see `mapNeedsGrow`) is
; what keeps the clustering paid for; the two decisions only make sense
; together.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The next slot after `i`.
;
; `(% (+ i 1) cap)` would do it in one expression; the compare is used
; instead because it is a branch rather than a division, and this is the
; innermost operation in the module.
;
; `pub` because `Intern` probes its own table the same way and had a
; byte-identical copy under its own name. One statement of the step.
(pub :: mapNextSlot (-> Int Int Int))

(pub fn (mapNextSlot i cap)
  (if (>= (+ i 1) cap)
    0
    (+ i 1)
  )
)

; WHY THE PROBE LOOPS TEST ONE CONDITION PER `if`.
;
; Not a compiler workaround, though it was: this file used to carry a
; note saying `&&` could not take a comparison operand and that a `Bool`
; must never be the value of an `if`. Both were real - the first emitted
; `and i64` over an `i1` and failed in `llc`, the second stored one byte
; into a word-sized slot and read seven bytes of stale frame back, which
; is how an early version of this file silently lost 11 of 40 keys - and
; both are fixed. `&&` and `||` now short-circuit, and a `Bool` is
; widened to a machine word wherever it is stored. See
; `tests/stdlib/130-booleans.ax`, which fails against a compiler with
; either bug.
;
; What is left is a genuine three-way distinction that no two-way
; conjunction expresses: a slot is empty, live, or a tombstone, and the
; three demand different actions. Empty ends the probe, live compares the
; key, and a tombstone continues past it - collapsing the last two into
; `(&& live (== key k))` would make a tombstone answer "not this slot"
; and stop the scan the same way empty does, losing every entry displaced
; past a removed one.
; WHY THE PROBE LOOPS CARRY THE ARRAYS AS PARAMETERS.
;
; Every accessor is two dependent loads - the header word holding the
; array's address, then the element - and the compiler cannot hoist the
; first out of a probe loop: every address here is an integer fed to
; `__load64`/`__store64`, so the optimiser has to assume any store may
; have rewritten any header word, and reloads the array base on every
; probe. Measured on the 10^6-key benchmark, that reloading was most of
; the gap to the Rust map. So each probe loop derives `keys`, `vals`,
; `states` and `cap` from the header once, at entry, and carries them as
; parameters - a tail-recursive loop keeps them in registers - and only
; the header words that the operation genuinely mutates (`len`, `used`)
; are touched through `m`. This is sound because nothing reallocates the
; arrays while a probe is in flight: only `mapRehash` replaces them, and
; no probe loop calls it.
; The slot holding `key`, or -1 if `key` is absent.
;
; Stops at the first empty slot: an entry is always placed at or after
; its home slot within an unbroken run of non-empty slots, so an empty
; slot proves absence. Tombstones are skipped rather than stopping the
; scan, which is the whole reason they exist - treating one as empty
; would make every entry displaced past a removed one unreachable.
;
; `left` counts down from `cap` so the scan is bounded even if the
; invariant "there is always at least one empty slot" is ever broken by a
; bug elsewhere in this file. Without it, that bug would present as a
; hang rather than as a wrong answer.
; The first probe is peeled out of the loop, here in the wrapper, and
; the loop is entered only from the second probe on. Not a stylistic
; unroll: the loop is self-recursive, and a self-recursive callee is
; the one thing the inliner will not inline, so the call was paid on
; *every* find. Peeling puts the common case - the home slot decides -
; on a path with no recursion anywhere, which inlines into every
; caller; the call survives only for keys that actually collide.
(:: mapFindSlot (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (mapFindSlot m key)
  (let (
    (cap (mapCap m))
    (states (memGetWord m 4))
    (keys (memGetWord m 2))
  )
    (let ((h (mapSlotOf key cap)))
      (let ((st (memGetByte states h)))
        (if (== st mapStateEmpty)
          (- 0 1)
          (if (== st mapStateLive)
            (if (== (memGetWord keys h) key)
              h
              (mapFindLoop
                states                keys                cap                key                (mapNextSlot h cap)                (- cap 1)
              )
            )
            (mapFindLoop
              states              keys              cap              key              (mapNextSlot h cap)              (- cap 1)
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

(:: mapFindLoop (-> Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))

(fn (mapFindLoop states keys cap key i left)
  (if (<= left 0)
    (- 0 1)
    (let ((st (memGetByte states i)))
      (if (== st mapStateEmpty)
        (- 0 1)
        (if (== st mapStateLive)
          (if (== (memGetWord keys i) key)
            i
            (mapFindLoop
              states              keys              cap              key              (mapNextSlot i cap)              (- left 1)
            )
          )
          ; A tombstone: keep going. Treating it as empty here
          ; would make every entry displaced past a removed one
          ; unreachable.
          (mapFindLoop
            states            keys            cap            key            (mapNextSlot i cap)            (- left 1)
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Reading
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: mapHas (-> Int Int Bool))

(pub fn (mapHas m key) (>= (mapFindSlot m key) 0))

; The value for `key`, or `dflt` if `key` is absent.
;
; Axiom's builtin `Option` does work end to end, but every `data` value
; is heap-boxed unconditionally, so an `Option`-returning `mapGet` would
; allocate 16 bytes on every successful lookup - in a bump allocator
; with no `free`, in the hottest operation of a compiler's symbol table.
; A caller-supplied default costs nothing and lets each call site pick a
; sentinel it knows cannot occur in its own value range; a caller with
; no such value uses `mapHas` first and pays the second probe only where
; it actually needs the distinction.
; IT ANSWERS `Int`, AND IT USED TO ANSWER A TYPE VARIABLE. That
; signature was `(-> Int Int a a)`, and the justification written here
; was: "`a` is witnessed by `dflt`, so this cast sits at a RETURN inside
; a signature that already carries the type." The first clause is true
; of the DEFAULT path and false of the one the cast is on. `dflt`
; witnesses what the caller wants back when the key is ABSENT; the cast
; is on the found path, where the word came out of the table and has
; nothing to do with `dflt`'s type. A `Map` carries no element type -
; that is what the handle convention IS - so the two are unrelated, and
; the signature let the caller name a type the table does not hold:
;
;     (mapInsert m 1 100000000)
;     (strLen (mapGet m 1 "absent"))
;
; `check` answered OK and the binary exited **139** - 100000000
; dereferenced as a String pointer. Measured 2026-08-25.
;
; So it answers `Int`, which is the truth about a machine word, and a
; caller who knows what the word is says so at the call:
; `mapGetStr` below. That is the shape `vecGet`/`vecGetStr` and
; `memGetWord`/`memGetWordStr` already had; `Map` was the one container
; whose reader still wore a polymorphic spelling. See `AX3040`, whose
; whole subject is a type variable the caller chooses and the callee
; cannot have produced.
;
; The default is caller-supplied rather than fixed at 0, and rather than
; returning `Option`, for the reason above: a heap-boxed `Option` on the
; hottest lookup in a compiler's symbol table.
(pub :: mapGet (-> Int Int Int Int))

(pub fn (mapGet m key dflt)
  (let ((i (mapFindSlot m key)))
    (if (< i 0)
      dflt
      ; `mapValAt` answers the raw word, and so does this. No cast: the
      ; declared result IS what the word is.
      (mapValAt m i)
    )
  )
)

; The value for `key` read as a `String`, or `dflt` if `key` is absent.
;
; The typed half of the pair, exactly as `vecGetStr` is to `vecGet`. It
; does NOT make the read safe - a `Map` still carries no element type,
; and reading a table of Ints through this segfaults just as before.
; What it buys is that the claim is made at the CALL SITE, in one word a
; reader can see, instead of being inferred from a default argument
; three tokens away.
;
; Spelled out rather than delegating to `mapGet`, which would need
; `(cast Int dflt)` - a cast at an ARGUMENT root, which classifies that
; value's evidence 0 and drops its retain (docs/memory-model.md
; MM-LIFE-2d). The cast here is at a RETURN, which is the safe position
; and the one `vecGetStr` uses.
(pub :: mapGetStr (-> Int Int String String))

(pub fn (mapGetStr m key dflt)
  (let ((i (mapFindSlot m key)))
    (if (< i 0)
      dflt
      (cast String (mapValAt m i))
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Writing
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; True when the table should be rehashed before another insert.
;
; Load factor 0.5, i.e. grow at `used * 2 >= cap`. Knuth's estimate for
; the expected probe count of an unsuccessful linear-probe search is
; (1 + 1/(1-a)^2)/2: 2.5 probes at a = 0.5, 6.1 at 0.7, 8.5 at 0.75.
; Since a miss is the common case for the workload this exists for - a
; type checker asking "is this name already bound in this scope" - paying
; 2x the memory to keep misses at 2.5 probes rather than 8.5 is the right
; way round. A chained map, or an SIMD-probed one like hashbrown, can
; afford 0.875; a scalar linear-probe table cannot.
;
; It is `used`, not `len`: see the module comment on tombstones.
(:: mapNeedsGrow (-> Int Bool))

(fn (mapNeedsGrow m) (>= (* (mapUsed m) 2) (mapCap m)))

; The capacity to rehash AT, which is not always twice the current one.
;
; `mapNeedsGrow` reads `used`, and `used` counts tombstones, so a table
; under insert-and-remove churn reaches the load factor with a live set
; that never moved. Doubling there grows the table for entries that do
; not exist: measured 2026-08-24 on a 256-entry window over 200,000
; inserts, the table climbed to about 524,288 slots and the process to
; 10 MB - a bounded live set with unbounded memory, which is exactly
; what P3 says must not happen and what nothing in this repository
; measured until `scripts/check-steady-state.sh`.
;
; So growth asks about `len` rather than `used`: when the LIVE entries
; would sit at a quarter load or less in the current table, the rehash
; happens at the SAME capacity, which drops every tombstone and grows
; nothing. Only a table that is genuinely full doubles.
;
; A quarter and not a half, because a half thrashes: rehashing at `cap`
; with `len * 2 == cap` leaves the table needing another rehash on the
; very next insert. At a quarter, `cap / 4` inserts must arrive before
; the next one can, so the rehash cost stays amortised O(1) per insert
; in both directions.
(:: mapRehashCap (-> Int Int))

(fn (mapRehashCap m)
  (if (<= (* (mapLen m) 4) (mapCap m))
    (mapCap m)
    (* (mapCap m) 2)
  )
)

; Insert or overwrite, growing first if the load factor demands it.
; Returns the handle - the same one, since the header is mutated in
; place; see `Vec`'s module comment for why it is returned anyway.
;
; Growth happens *before* the insert, not after, so the table a caller
; gets back is never over its load factor. Growing after would let a
; single insert push `used` to `cap` and leave the next lookup's probe
; with no empty slot to stop at.
(pub :: mapInsert (-> Int Int a Int))

(pub fn (mapInsert m key value)
  {
    (if (mapNeedsGrow m)
      (mapRehash m (mapRehashCap m))
      m
    )
    (mapInsertNoGrow m key value)
  }
)

; Insert or overwrite, assuming there is room. Returns the handle.
;
; The scan carries `firstTomb`, the first tombstone it passed, because
; the two things an insert must get right pull in opposite directions: it
; must reuse a tombstone if it can, to stop `used` from ratcheting up
; under an insert/remove workload; but it must not reuse one before
; proving the key is not already live further along the run, or the map
; would hold two entries for one key and `mapLen` would be wrong. So the
; scan always runs to the first empty slot, and only then decides between
; the remembered tombstone and the empty slot it stopped on.
; The first probe is peeled for the reason given on `mapFindSlot`: the
; recursive loop cannot be inlined, and most inserts are decided at the
; home slot. A tombstone there falls into the loop remembering it, so
; the reuse rule is exactly the loop's.
(:: mapInsertNoGrow (-> Int Int a Int))

(fn (mapInsertNoGrow m key value)
  (let (
    (cap (mapCap m))
    (keys (memGetWord m 2))
    (vals (memGetWord m 3))
    (states (memGetWord m 4))
  )
    (let ((h (mapSlotOf key cap)))
      (let ((st (memGetByte states h)))
        (if (== st mapStateEmpty)
          (mapFillAt
            m            keys            vals            states            h            key            value            1
          )
          (if (== st mapStateLive)
            (if (== (memGetWord keys h) key)
              {
                (mapDropVal m h)
                (memSetWord vals h value)
                m
              }
              (mapInsertLoop
                m                keys                vals                states                cap                key                value                (mapNextSlot h cap)                (- 0 1)                (- cap 1)
              )
            )
            (mapInsertLoop
              m              keys              vals              states              cap              key              value              (mapNextSlot h cap)              h              (- cap 1)
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

(:: mapInsertLoop (-> Int Int Int Int Int Int a Int Int Int Int))

(fn (mapInsertLoop m keys vals states cap key value i firstTomb left)
  (if (<= left 0)
    m
    (let ((state (memGetByte states i)))
      (if (== state mapStateEmpty)
        ; End of the run. Prefer the remembered tombstone, so that
        ; insert/remove churn reuses slots instead of consuming a
        ; fresh empty one on every cycle.
        (if (< firstTomb 0)
          (mapFillAt
            m            keys            vals            states            i            key            value            1
          )
          (mapFillAt
            m            keys            vals            states            firstTomb            key            value            0
          )
        )
        (if (== state mapStateLive)
          (if (== (memGetWord keys i) key)
            ; Overwrite: `len` and `used` are both unchanged,
            ; which is the property the golden test pins down - a
            ; map that counted an overwrite as an insertion would
            ; grow without bound under repeated assignment to one
            ; key.
            {
              (mapDropVal m i)
              (memSetWord vals i value)
              m
            }
            (mapInsertLoop
              m              keys              vals              states              cap              key              value              (mapNextSlot i cap)              firstTomb              (- left 1)
            )
          )
          ; A tombstone. Remember the first one and keep scanning:
          ; reusing it now, before the run has been searched to its
          ; end, would risk storing a second entry for a key that
          ; is already live further along.
          (mapInsertLoop
            m            keys            vals            states            cap            key            value            (mapNextSlot i cap)            (if (< firstTomb 0)
              i
              firstTomb
            )            (- left 1)
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; Write a new entry into slot `i` and account for it.
;
; `consumesEmpty` distinguishes the two callers: filling a previously
; empty slot raises `used`, filling a tombstone does not, because the
; tombstone was already counted when it was created. Getting this
; backwards is invisible in every functional test and shows up only as a
; table that either grows too eagerly or, much worse, never grows and
; eventually has no empty slot for a probe to terminate on.
;
; It is an `Int` flag (1 or 0) rather than a `Bool` on purpose. A `Bool`
; would be correct here - a `Bool` parameter is passed as `i64` and
; testing one in an `if` condition is a path that works - but this module
; keeps `Bool` confined to the two functions that return one directly,
; because the failure mode described above the probe loops is silent and
; stack-dependent, and this is the layer everything else is going to
; trust.
(:: mapFillAt (-> Int Int Int Int Int Int a Int Int))

(fn (mapFillAt m keys vals states i key value consumesEmpty)
  {
    (memSetWord keys i key)
    (memSetWord vals i value)
    (memPutByte states i mapStateLive)
    (memSetWord m 0 (+ (mapLen m) 1))
    (if (== consumesEmpty 1)
      (memSetWord m 5 (+ (mapUsed m) 1))
      m
    )
  }
)

; Delete `key`. Returns the handle.
;
; The slot becomes a tombstone, not empty: emptying it would truncate
; every probe run passing through it and orphan the entries after it.
; `len` drops, `used` does not - the slot is still not available to a
; probe as a stopping point, and only a rehash reclaims it.
(pub :: mapRemove (-> Int Int Int))

(pub fn (mapRemove m key)
  (let ((i (mapFindSlot m key)))
    (if (< i 0)
      m
      ; The slot's value is dropped HERE rather than left for the
      ; rehash to notice: a tombstone is not copied forward, so a
      ; value left in place would be released neither by the move
      ; nor by the caller. Zeroing it is also what keeps the
      ; array-form walk honest if the table is freed before it
      ; ever rehashes.
      {
        (mapDropVal m i)
        (memPutByte (memGetWord m 4) i mapStateTomb)
        (memSetWord m 0 (- (mapLen m) 1))
        m
      }
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Rehashing
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Rebuild `m`'s table at `newCap` slots. Returns the handle.
;
; The old arrays are read while the header already points at the new
; ones, so the three old addresses are captured into `let` bindings
; first. Every live entry is re-placed by `mapPlaceLoop`, a probe
; specialised to what a rehash target is: no slot is ever a tombstone
; and no key arrives twice, so the scan needs no key comparison, no
; tombstone memory, and no load-factor check - `newCap` is at least
; twice the old `cap`, so the entries fit.
;
; `len` and `used` are written once, at the end, from the count of
; entries actually placed - after a rehash the two are equal by
; construction, because tombstones are simply not copied. That is the
; only way the space a removed entry occupies ever comes back, and it
; is why `mapRemove` can be O(1) and still leave the table healthy.
(:: mapRehash (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (mapRehash m newCap)
  (let (
    (oldCap (mapCap m))
    (oldKeys (memGetWord m 2))
    (oldVals (memGetWord m 3))
    (oldStates (memGetWord m 4))
  )
    (let (
      (newKeys (memAlloc (* newCap 8)))
      (newVals (memAlloc (* newCap 8)))
      (newStates (memAlloc newCap))
    )
      {
        (__retain newKeys)
        (__retain newVals)
        (__retain newStates)
        (if (mapOwnsVals m)
          (memMarkArray newVals)
          newVals
        )
        (memSetWord m 1 newCap)
        (memSetWord m 2 newKeys)
        (memSetWord m 3 newVals)
        (memSetWord m 4 newStates)
        (let ((n (mapReinsertLoop
          newKeys          newVals          newStates          newCap          oldKeys          oldVals          oldStates          oldCap          0          0
        )))
          {
            (memSetWord m 0 n)
            (memSetWord m 5 n)
            ; A rehash MOVES every live value's share into the new
            ; array and drops the TOMBSTONED ones, so the old array
            ; may not be released as an array - it would spend the
            ; moved shares a second time. `mapRemove` has already
            ; handed back what it removed, so nothing is owed here.
            (memMarkLeaf oldVals)
            (__release oldKeys)
            (__release oldVals)
            (__release oldStates)
            m
          }
        )
      }
    )
  )
)

; Walk the old arrays, placing every live entry into the new ones.
; Returns how many were placed.
(:: mapReinsertLoop (-> Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))

(fn (mapReinsertLoop newKeys newVals newStates newCap oldKeys oldVals oldStates oldCap i cnt)
  (if (>= i oldCap)
    cnt
    (if (== (memGetByte oldStates i) mapStateLive)
      {
        (mapPlaceLoop
          newKeys          newVals          newStates          newCap          (memGetWord oldKeys i)          (memGetWord oldVals i)          (mapSlotOf (memGetWord oldKeys i) newCap)          newCap
        )
        (mapReinsertLoop
          newKeys          newVals          newStates          newCap          oldKeys          oldVals          oldStates          oldCap          (+ i 1)          (+ cnt 1)
        )
      }
      (mapReinsertLoop
        newKeys        newVals        newStates        newCap        oldKeys        oldVals        oldStates        oldCap        (+ i 1)        cnt
      )
    )
  )
)

; Place `key`/`value` at the first empty slot of a probe starting at
; `i`. Only correct for a rehash target - a table with no tombstones
; receiving each key exactly once - which is what lets it skip the
; key-comparison and tombstone logic the general insert carries.
;
; `left` bounds the scan for the same reason `mapFindLoop`'s does: a
; full table here means a bug elsewhere, and dropping the entry is a
; wrong answer a test can see, where an unbounded scan is a hang.
(:: mapPlaceLoop (-> Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int Int))

(fn (mapPlaceLoop keys vals states cap key value i left)
  (if (<= left 0)
    0
    (if (== (memGetByte states i) mapStateEmpty)
      {
        (memSetWord keys i key)
        (memSetWord vals i value)
        (memPutByte states i mapStateLive)
        0
      }
      (mapPlaceLoop
        keys        vals        states        cap        key        value        (mapNextSlot i cap)        (- left 1)
      )
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Iteration
;
; A table you cannot enumerate is not a table. Until 2026-09-03 this
; module exported `mapLen` and `mapCap` - a loop bound with nothing to
; read inside it - and the three slot accessors were private, so no
; caller outside this file could name the contents of a `Map` at all.
; The `mapSumKeys`/`mapSumVals` pair below is the evidence: its own
; comment says the two sums "pin down a small map's contents well
; enough to test with", which is a workaround written because the
; obvious thing was unavailable, not a design.
;
; THE CURSOR, NOT A CALLBACK. `mapLiveFrom` answers a slot index and
; the caller reads it with `mapKeyAt`/`mapValAt`, rather than this
; module taking a function and calling it per entry. A callback would
; allocate a closure record per iteration site and would have to
; decide what a caller returning early means; a cursor is a word, is
; allocation-free, and composes with `while`, with early `return`, and
; with a caller that wants only keys or only values.
;
;   (let ((mut c (mapLiveFrom m 0)))
;     (while (isSomeSlot c)
;       { ... (mapKeyAt m (slotOf c)) ... (set c (mapLiveFrom m (+ (slotOf c) 1))) }))
;
; ORDER IS SLOT ORDER, WHICH IS TO SAY UNDEFINED - the same statement
; the "Bulk reads" section below makes, and for the same reason: slot
; order changes on every rehash. Two iterations of a table that has
; not been written to in between agree; nothing else is promised, and
; a caller that needs an order must impose one (`Vec.vecSort` over the
; keys).
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The first live slot at or after `i`, or `None` when the table has no
; live slot from there on. `(mapLiveFrom m 0)` starts an iteration;
; `(mapLiveFrom m (+ prev 1))` continues one.
;
; Self-tail-recursive over `cap`, so it is a loop after TCO - the same
; shape `mapSumKeysFrom` has carried over the same range since this
; module was written.
(pub :: mapLiveFrom (-> Int Int (Option Int)))

(pub fn (mapLiveFrom m i)
  (if (>= i (mapCap m))
    None
    (if (== (mapStateAt m i) mapStateLive)
      (Some i)
      (mapLiveFrom m (+ i 1))
    )
  )
)

; Every live key, and every live value, in one shared slot order: the
; `j`th key and the `j`th value came out of the same slot, so the two
; vectors zip. Both are freshly allocated and the caller owns them.
;
; These allocate where the cursor does not, and both are here rather
; than only one of them because a caller that wants pairs otherwise
; walks the table twice.
(pub :: mapKeys (-> Int (Vec Int)))

(pub fn (mapKeys m) (mapCollect m 0 (vecWithCapacity (mapLen m)) 1))

; Every live value, in the same slot order `mapKeys` uses, so the two
; vectors zip element for element.
(pub :: mapValues (-> Int (Vec Int)))

(pub fn (mapValues m) (mapCollect m 0 (vecWithCapacity (mapLen m)) 0))

; The walk both collectors share. `keys` picks which array is read, so
; the traversal exists once: two copies of a probe loop is how this
; module's own `mapNextSlot` comment says `Intern` went wrong.
(:: mapCollect (-> Int Int (Vec Int) Int (Vec Int)))

(fn (mapCollect m i out keys)
  (match (mapLiveFrom m i)
    ((None) out)
    ((Some s)
      {
        (vecPush out (if (== keys 1)
          (mapKeyAt m s)
          (mapValAt m s)
        ))
        (mapCollect m (+ s 1) out keys)
      }
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Bulk reads
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The sum of every live value.
;
; Order-independent on purpose: a `Map` has no defined iteration order -
; it is the slot order, which changes on every rehash - so a
; position-sensitive digest like `Vec.vecHash` would be a test that
; asserts an implementation detail and breaks when the load factor
; changes. A sum over an unordered collection is exactly as strong a
; check as the collection's semantics allow.
(pub :: mapSumVals (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (mapSumVals m) (mapSumValsFrom m 0 0))

(:: mapSumValsFrom (-> Int Int Int Int))

(fn (mapSumValsFrom m i acc)
  (if (>= i (mapCap m))
    acc
    (mapSumValsFrom
      m      (+ i 1)      (if (== (mapStateAt m i) mapStateLive)
        (+ acc (mapValAt m i))
        acc
      )
    )
  )
)

; The sum of every live key. Together with `mapSumVals` and `mapLen`
; this pins down a small map's contents well enough to test with.
(pub :: mapSumKeys (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (mapSumKeys m) (mapSumKeysFrom m 0 0))

(:: mapSumKeysFrom (-> Int Int Int Int))

(fn (mapSumKeysFrom m i acc)
  (if (>= i (mapCap m))
    acc
    (mapSumKeysFrom
      m      (+ i 1)      (if (== (mapStateAt m i) mapStateLive)
        (+ acc (mapKeyAt m i))
        acc
      )
    )
  )
)
